# 34. Writing an extension

This chapter writes a package from an empty directory: `hello`, which
provides one module of the same name. It has a crate, a contract and five
Rust functions, and it is small enough to read whole. The finished package
is in the `hql` repository under
[`tests/packages/hello/`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/tree/main/tests/packages/hello),
where the tests of `hql` build and load it.

A package needs a Rust toolchain: `cargo`, installed with
[rustup](https://rustup.rs). It needs no knowledge of how `hql` is built
beyond one module of its crate, `hql::ext`.

## The parts of a package

```text
hello/
├── Cargo.toml                the crate, and what package it is
├── modules/
│   └── hello/mod.hql         the contract: declarations without bodies
└── src/
    └── lib.rs                the extension: one Rust function per name
```

| Part | Written in | Says |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Cargo.toml` | TOML | the crate's name, that its library is loadable, and the package's name and modules |
| `modules/` | HQL | what a program may call, with types and doc strings |
| `src/` | Rust | what each call computes |

## The order of work

1. [The crate](the-crate.hmd): a `Cargo.toml` that makes a crate a package.
2. [The contract](contracts.hmd): the module file a program is checked
   against.
3. [Natives](natives.hmd): a Rust function per declared name, the table
   that lists them, and the extension that exports the table.
4. [Values and errors](values-and-errors.hmd): what a function receives and
   returns, records of the package's own types, and failures.
5. [Functions and the context](lambdas-and-context.hmd): calling a function
   the program passed in, and what else the running program offers.

With the three files written, one command builds the package and installs
it, and a program imports it:

```sh
hql package install --path hello
```

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

hello.greet("Ada")
```

```text
"Hello, Ada!"
```

While a package is being written, a workspace names its crate instead, and
`hql` rebuilds it whenever its source is newer than the last build
([[36-installing-packages#a-package-under-development|chapter 36]]). The
programs of this chapter run that way.

## The pages of this chapter

| Page | What it covers |
| --- | --- |
| [The crate](the-crate.hmd) | `Cargo.toml`: the crate type, the `hql` dependency, `[package.metadata.hql]` |
| [The contract](contracts.hmd) | the module file, its types, its declarations and their doc strings |
| [Natives](natives.hmd) | the signature of a native function, the table of operations, `hql::extension!` |
| [Values and errors](values-and-errors.hmd) | `Value`, records, named arguments and defaults, failures and panics |
| [Functions and the context](lambdas-and-context.hmd) | applying a function argument, and `EvalCx` |

